    {
      "id": "hook",
      "type": "hook",
      "title": "Thirty students, one variable",
      "body": "Marks for a whole class, prices for a week, scores for every round. You can’t make a variable for each one. An **array** keeps many values of the same type in one row of numbered boxes.\n\nIn five minutes you’ll walk through an array box by box, meet the famous crash every beginner hits, and use one yourself."
    },
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this program print?",
      "code": "public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] marks = {70, 85, 90}; //~ An array of 3 ints. The positions (indexes) are 0, 1 and 2.\n        int sum = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < marks.length; i++) { //~ marks.length is 3, so i goes 0, 1, 2 and stops before 3.\n            sum += marks[i]; //~ marks[i] reads the box at position i and adds it to sum.\n        }\n        System.out.println(sum / marks.length); //~ 245 / 3 with ints is 81. The .67 is dropped.\n    }\n}",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "245",
          "why": "That’s the sum. The last line divides it by the length."
        },
        {
          "text": "Crashes: ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException",
          "why": "i < marks.length stops at i = 2, the last valid index, so nothing goes out of bounds."
        },
        {
          "text": "81",
          "why": "Right: 70 + 85 + 90 = 245, and 245 / 3 as ints is 81."
        },
        {
          "text": "81.67",
          "why": "sum and marks.length are both ints, so the division drops the decimals."
        }
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
