    {
      "id": "ideas",
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "How arrays work",
      "points": [
        "Create one with values `int[] a = {4, 8, 15}` or empty with a size: `new int[n]` (filled with 0s).",
        "Indexes run from **0** to **length − 1**. `a[0]` is the first box and `a[a.length - 1]` is the last.",
        "The standard walk: `for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++)`. Note the `<`, not `<=`.",
        "An array’s size is fixed when you make it. Reading `a[a.length]` crashes the program."
      ],
      "pattern": "int[] a = new int[5];\nfor (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {\n    a[i] = i * i;\n}"
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "A classic trap. What does this print?",
      "code": "public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] a = {4, 8, 15};\n        System.out.println(a[a.length]);\n    }\n}",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "It doesn't compile",
          "why": "The compiler can’t know the index is bad; the error happens when it runs."
        },
        {
          "text": "15",
          "why": "The last index is length - 1 = 2. a[3] doesn’t exist."
        },
        {
          "text": "0",
          "why": "Java doesn’t invent a value for a missing box; it throws an exception."
        },
        {
          "text": "Crashes: ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException",
          "why": "Yes. a.length is 3, but the indexes are only 0 to 2, so Java stops the program."
        }
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
